mechanism:
In NVPTXTargetLowering::getTgtMemIntrinsic, the case for Intrinsic::nvvm_ldu_global_i/_f/_p does:

    Info.align = cast<ConstantInt>(I.getArgOperand(1))->getMaybeAlignValue();

arg index 1 is the 'pointer alignment' operand. The matching intrinsic definitions in IntrinsicsNVVM.td (lines 2169-2172) are:

    let IntrProperties = [IntrReadMem, IntrArgMemOnly, IntrNoCallback, IntrWillReturn, NoCapture<ArgIndex<0>>] in {
      def int_nvvm_ldu_global_i : Intrinsic<[llvm_anyint_ty], [llvm_anyptr_ty, llvm_i32_ty]>;
      def int_nvvm_ldu_global_f : Intrinsic<[llvm_anyfloat_ty], [llvm_anyptr_ty, llvm_i32_ty]>;
      def int_nvvm_ldu_global_p : Intrinsic<[llvm_anyptr_ty], [llvm_anyptr_ty, llvm_i32_ty]>;
    }

There is NO ImmArg<ArgIndex<1>>, so the verifier permits a non-constant (runtime) i32 align argument. The unchecked cast<ConstantInt> then fails: in an asserts build it triggers 'cast<Ty>() argument of incompatible type!' (Casting.h:572); in a release build it is UB / reads garbage as the alignment. getTgtMemIntrinsic runs during SelectionDAGBuilder::visitTargetIntrinsic before any constant-folding could rescue it, so a non-constant operand reaches the cast directly.

