mechanism:
combineMulWide folds (sign_extend (shl nsw x, C)) -> MUL_WIDE_SIGNED(x, 2^C). It builds the multiplier in the *narrow* FromVT:

  SDValue RHS = Op.getOperand(1);
  if (Op.getOpcode() == ISD::SHL) {
    const auto ShiftAmt = Op.getConstantOperandVal(1);
    const auto MulVal = APInt(FromVT.getSizeInBits(), 1) << ShiftAmt;   // <-- 2^C in FromVT
    RHS = DCI.DAG.getConstant(MulVal, DL, FromVT);
  }
  return DCI.DAG.getNode(Opcode, DL, ToVT, Op.getOperand(0), RHS);

When ExtOpcode==SIGN_EXTEND (Opcode==MUL_WIDE_SIGNED) and ShiftAmt == FromVT.getSizeInBits()-1, MulVal has the top bit of FromVT set: 0x8000 for i16, 0x80000000 for i32. mul.wide.s16/s32 (smul_wide in NVPTXInstrInfo.td:1037-1040) SIGN-EXTENDS both operands, so this constant is interpreted as -2^(bits-1), not +2^(bits-1). The multiplier's sign is flipped, so the whole result is negated.

This is the exact sibling of the round-1 combineMulWide bug ('fabricates a constant in the narrow type whose top bit is set, then feeds mul.wide.s which sign-extends, negating the value'). PR #168986 fixed the constant's *type width* (ToVT -> FromVT) but left the *sign* wrong at the boundary shift amount; before that fix the constant was built in ToVT and never had the high bit of FromVT set there. TryMULWIDECombine (the other path) avoids this by checking Val.isSignedIntN(OptSize), which rejects 2^(bits-1); combineMulWide has no such guard.

Concrete (i16->i32): x = -1 (i16). shl nsw i16 -1, 15: -1<<15 = 0x8000 = -32768 in i16, and nsw holds (all 15 shifted-out bits equal the result sign bit). sext to i32 = -32768. But emitted mul.wide.s16(%rs=-1, -32768) = (-1)*(-32768) = +32768. Wrong: 32768 vs -32768.
Verified vs -O0 reference, which emits 'shl.b16; cvt.s32.s16' (correct -32768). Same defect for i32->i64 with shl 31: mul.wide.s32(x, -2147483648); x=-1 gives +2147483648 instead of -2147483648.

