mechanism:
LowerINT_TO_FP lowers int->bf16 by converting to f32 first and then rounding f32->bf16:
  if (Op.getValueType() == MVT::bf16) {
    return DAG.getNode(ISD::FP_ROUND, Loc, MVT::bf16,
        DAG.getNode(Op.getOpcode(), Loc, MVT::f32, Op.getOperand(0)),
        DAG.getIntPtrConstant(0, Loc, true));
  }
This is two roundings: integer -> f32 (round-to-nearest to 24 mantissa bits), then f32 -> bf16 (round-to-nearest to 8 mantissa bits). A correctly-rounded sitofp/uitofp must round the exact integer directly to bf16 (single rounding). Double rounding can yield a bf16 one ULP away from the correctly-rounded result when the first rounding lands on a value that then ties (or crosses a halfway point) differently in the second rounding. Note the backend already knows the correct fix (round-inexact-to-odd) and uses it for f64->bf16 fptrunc in LowerFP_ROUND (expandRoundInexactToOdd), but the int->bf16 path here does a naive double round. Confirmed emitted PTX on sm_80/ptx70 for `sitofp i64 -> bfloat`:
  cvt.rn.f32.s64 %r1, %rd1;
  cvt.rn.bf16.f32 %rs1, %r1;
whereas sm_90/ptx78 emits the correct single conversion `cvt.rn.bf16.s64`. Same double-round applies to uitofp and to i16/i32/i64 sources whose exact value is not representable in f32.

